"aicom/docs/localization-glossary.md at main · alexar76/aicom · GitHub (https://github.com/alexar76/aicom/blob/main/docs/localization-glossary.md)\nciteturn22search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; `HISTOR`, `MTL/1`, `MCP Trust Label`, `STH`, `did:key`, the method ids (`tool-set-observation`, `tool-def-pattern-scan`, `tool-set-continuity`, `name-threat-match`), the verdict tokens `pass` / `fail` / `inconclusive`, the tiers `block` / `advise`, reason codes (`MTL-SUBJ-003` …), HTTP statuses (`http-401`) and the capability ids (`histor.check@v1`) are identifiers and never translated. ... MTL/1 §9.2 forbids rendering any label outcome as safe, secure, audited, certified, approved, trusted or an unqualified verified — and the same holds for their equivalents: RU «безопасный / проверенный / одобренный / доверенный», ES «seguro / verificado / aprobado / de confianza», FR «sûr / sécurisé / vérifié / approuvé / de confiance», ZH «安全 / 已验证 / 已认证 / 可信».\nMust not be rendered as «постквантовая безопасность» / «seguridad post-cuántica» / «sécurité post-quantique» / 后量子安全 — that is phase 3, and conflating them overstates what is deployed.\n\n## HISTOR terms (transparency log of MCP tool definitions)\n\nAdded with `histor/` (2026-09-23). `HISTOR`, `MTL/1`, `MCP Trust Label`, `STH`, `did:key`, the method ids (`tool-set-observation`, `tool-def-pattern-scan`, `tool-set-continuity`, `name-threat-match`), the verdict tokens `pass` / `fail` / `inconclusive`, the tiers `block` / `advise`, reason codes (`MTL-SUBJ-003` …), HTTP statuses (`http-401`) and the capability ids (`histor.check@v1`) are identifiers and never translated. Log vocabulary follows the established Certificate Transparency renderings (RFC 6962 / RFC 9162), web-verified below.\n\nVocabulary rule that outranks translation. MTL/1 §9.2 forbids rendering any label outcome as safe, secure, audited, certified, approved, trusted or an unqualified verified — and the same holds for their equivalents: RU «безопасный / проверенный / одобренный / доверенный», ES «seguro / verificado / aprobado / de confianza», FR «sûr / sécurisé / vérifié / approuvé / de confiance», ZH «安全 / 已验证 / 已认证 / 可信». consistency proof  | доказательство согласованности  | prueba de consistencia  | preuve de cohérence  | 一致性证明  | Shows a later head extends an earlier one: nothing removed or rewritten.\nappend-only  | только на добавление  | solo de adición  | en ajout seul  | 仅追加  | \ntool definition  | определение инструмента  | definición de herramienta  | définition d’outil  | 工具定义  | The name + description + schemas an MCP server advertises.\ntool set  | набор инструментов  | conjunto de herramientas  | ensemble d’outils  | 工具集  | Everything `tools/list` returned, drained across pages.\nadvertised (definitions)  | объявленные  | anunciadas  | annoncées  | 公布的  | What the server says to every client. Never «заявленные как безопасные».\nlabel (MTL)  | метка  | etiqueta  | étiquette  | 标签  | One signed MTL/1 document. The type names `MCPTrustLabel` / `VerificationVerdict` stay Latin.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions | VibeLeaderboard (https://www.vibeleaderboard.ai/app/d8a7cafb-d59e-470a-9207-e89df4720091)\nciteturn22search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; histor.modelmarket.dev\n\n← Back to Tools\n\nIndex / tool\n\n# HISTOR Open Dataset of MCP Tool Definitions\n\nhistor.modelmarket.dev\n\nVisit histor.modelmarket.dev\n\nCategory\n    Cybersecurity\n\nRank\n    \n\nNo. 2267Tools index\n\nPrevious survey · No. 2094 · 30 October 2026\n\nPricing\n    Open Source\n\nType\n    TOOL\n\nUse case\n    Security & Identity\n\nDate\n    Published Oct 22, 2026\n\n## About\n\nMonthly snapshots of a public transparency log covering remote MCP servers in the official registry and npm and PyPI MCP packages. The files hold the tool definitions each server served, every recorded change field by field, pattern scan matches, and advisory classifier readings. A signed manifest lists checksums so users can verify files, and the data is released under CC BY 4.0.\n\n## What it does\n\nThe site says HISTOR reads the tool list of every remote server in the official MCP registry each day and runs each new npm and PyPI MCP package version in a sandbox. It presents its pattern matches and classifier flags as leads worth reading, not as proof that a server is malicious.\n\n### Stated on the product site\n\nLicense\n    CC BY 4.0\n\nRelease cadence\n    A full snapshot published as files once a month\n\nVerification\n    A manifest of file checksums, signed with Ed25519\n\nMirrors\n    Hugging Face with one tag per snapshot, Zenodo with a DOI per snapshot\n\nCoverage limit\n    Servers that require a key show less or nothing to the anonymous client\n\nCode link\n    The page links a GitHub repository for the project\n\n### Not stated on the site\n\n  * The page does not name the LLM behind the advisory classifier readings.\n  * The page does not say what the sandbox run of a package records beyond its tool list.\n  * How server operators' opt-out requests are handled beyond opening an issue is not described on the page.\n\nWritten from the product site at histor.modelmarket.dev.\n\n## Tags\n\nmcp dataset transparency-log tool-definitions supply-chain security--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log — OpenAI Suite MCPs (https://openaisuite.com/mcp/io.github.alexar76/histor/)\nciteturn22search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; Check, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers. ... Public HISTOR instance to query (default https://histor.modelmarket.dev).\n\n# HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nio.github.alexar76/historSource repository\n\nVersion 0.5.2latest catalog version Connection Packagepypi Published Oct 31, 2026catalog Updated Oct 31, 2026catalog\n\nOverview\n\n## What this server provides\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nInstall\n\n## Packages\n\n1\n\nPackage 1 pypi\n\naimarket-histor 0.5.2\n\nRuntime `Not declared`Transport `stdio`\n\n  * HISTOR_MCP_REMOTE\n\nPublic HISTOR instance to query (default https://histor.modelmarket.dev). Pass --local to serve your own log from HISTOR_DATA_DIR instead.\n\nView server metadata\n\nServer details\n\nNamespace\n    io.github.alexar76\n\nStatus\n    active\n\nVersion\n    0.5.2\n\nRepository\n    github\n\nFormat schema\n    JSON Schema--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://alexar76.github.io/histor/)\nciteturn22search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; ref=mcp-registry changed 2026-11-01 ASPERN: counterparty checks for agents · org.aspern/aspern 40 tools 1 block-tier 10 advise-tier https://aspern.org/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Multicines Ortega · com.multicinesortega/cartelera 3 tools https://multicinesortega.com/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Sirenic · eu.sirenic/sirenic 120 tools 472 advise-tier https://api.sirenic.eu/mcp changed 2026-11-03 Summer · ai.gosummer/summer 234 tools 29 advise-tier https://api.gosummer.ai/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Speedbot Autonomous Work Network · io.github.ulasarslan6262-ui/speedbot 145 tools 1 block-tier 20 advise-tier https://speedbot.dev/mcp changed 2026-11-01 Straelo Relay · com.straelo/relay 15 tools https://relay.straelo.com/mcp/changed 2026-11-03 tgden — Telegram catalog & search · io.github.JustJuice55/telegram-catalog 6 tools https://tgden.com/api/mcp changed 2026-11-02 nanoodle · io.github.nanoodlecom/nanoodle-mcp 13 tools 5 advise-tier https://mcp.nanoodle.com/mcp changed 2026-11-03 uk.minia2a/minia2a-mcp-remote 1846 tools 3 block-tier 31 advise-tier https://minia2a.uk/mcp changed 2026-11-04 AgentDrives · com.agentdrives/directory 37 tools 23 advise-tier https://agentdrives.com/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Versum Agent Assistant · io.github.sudzikcoin/versum-agent-assistant 15 tools 1 advise-tier https://versum.suverse.io/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Flaim Fantasy · app.flaim/mcp 17 tools 4 advise-tier https://api.flaim.app/mcp changed 2026-11-04 com.jojapi/product-barcode-api 8 tools 1 advise-tier https://mcp.jojapi.com/product-barcode-api changed 2026-11-04 SwarmMemo · com.swarmmemo/bulletin 21 tools 5 advise-tier https://swarmmemo.com/mcp changed 2026-11-01 Tanod · dev.tanod/tanod 317 tools 3 block-tier 41 advise-tier https://tanod.dev/mcp changed 2026-11-03 EventTrader MCP · com.cymetica/event-trader-mcp 176 tools 120 advise-tier https://cymetica.com/mcp/v1 changed 2026-11-04 com.thefomite/fomite 20 tools 2 advise-tier https://thefomite.com/mcp changed 2026-11-04 AgentDrives · com.agentdrives/directory 9 tools 1 advise-tier https://agentdrives.com/api/mcp changed 2026-11-04 HotelRefund Price Tracker · net.hotelrefund/price-tracker 11 tools 2 advise-tier https://hotelrefund.net/mcp changed 2026-11-04 Remote Camera · moda.ai/remote-camera 2 tools 1 advise-tier https://www.ai.moda/mcp-servers/remote-camera/mcp changed 2026-11-04 ...     curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\ ... The badge shows the date your definitions were pinned or last changed, and links to the full history.\n\nOther logs, the same servers\n\n## Outside checks\n\nHISTOR is not the only public record of MCP tool definitions. Where another log publishes its reads, the two are compared on the same servers and days, and the result is published both ways, with HISTOR's own misses.\n\n### ScraperWatch\n\nA daily diff of a fixed panel of about 3,000 remote endpoints. HISTOR compares each of their published days with its own reads, and ScraperWatch shows the result on its page under \"Outside check\". The first comparison found a robots.txt parsing limit on their side and a handshake regression on HISTOR's; both were fixed the same day.\n\nscraperwatch.com/mcp-changes\n/datasets/peers/scraperwatch/latest\n\n### heldfast\n\nAn open daily record of the tool definitions of the servers in the official MCP registry. Of the 23,190 servers both logs recorded on 26 October 2026, 21,215 had every field in both; on those, 97.9% of servers and 99.1% of tools were identical byte for byte, and each difference has a stated cause: package versions, read times, failed reads, a first page only, an older protocol.\n\ngithub.com/rufat325/heldfast\n/datasets/interop-digests-2026-10-31\n\nFor clients, registries and server authors\n\n## Use it\n\nBefore your agent trusts a server, send the tools it received. The answer is signed by the log's did:key and names the tree head it was given under.\n    \n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …tools/list result… ],\"contribute\":true}'\n    # -> {\"type\":\"histor.check/v1\",\"match\":\"same\"|\"different\"|\"previously-observed\"|…,\n    #     \"observed\":{\"unchangedSince\":\"…\",\"changes\":0}, \"patternScan\":{…}, \"signature\":{…}}\n    \n    # README badge for your server\n    [![MCP tool defs](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/badge/<id>.svg)](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/s/<id>)\n    \n    # Atom feed of every observed change\n    https://histor.modelmarket.dev/feed.xml\n\nWARDEN checks definitions at connect\n\nHISTOR remembers what was advertised\n\nTHEMIS admits at publish\n\nHub lists histor.check@v1\n\nAWR the signed format\n\n## Questions\n\nIs this a marketplace listing?\n\nNo. The server list is the log's index: every remote endpoint in the official MCP registry, with what HISTOR could observe about it. Nothing here is for sale and nothing is ranked by quality.\n\nDoes HISTOR tell me a server is safe?\n\nNo, and it never will. It tells you what a server advertised, whether that changed, and which published patterns matched the text. Whether to trust the server is your decision — HISTOR makes sure you decide on the same facts as everyone else.\n\nWhy are so many servers \"not observed\"?\n\nMost remote MCP endpoints require a login (they answer 401 to anyone without a key), some use the legacy SSE transport or need per-user values in the URL, and some are simply down. They stay on the list with the reason, so the numbers never hide them.\n\nHow do I check a server I already use?\n\nOpen the Check tab, paste the server's endpoint and the tools/list result your client received, and press Check. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog request: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (staging, 10qps list) - Participate - Witness-network mailing lists (https://lists.witness-network.org/mailman3/hyperkitty/list/participate%40lists.witness-network.org/thread/J5ZRRQ2EQRVIFF7LL23TWMTL4AVCLYNZ/)\nciteturn22search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week; ### Log request: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (staging, 10qps list)\n\n### Log request: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (staging, 10qps list)\n\n## Александр Артамохов\n\n30 Oct '26\n\n10:51 a.m.\n\nHello, I would like to request that HISTOR's log be added to the staging witness network, on the 10qps list. vkey histor.modelmarket.dev/log+ad3ca066+AfXpkWqXVW93sChXnhKsNzvGLTFAAredl1jLlEjbr4wU qpd 144 contact alexar76@rambler.ru Origin line: histor.modelmarket.dev/log (equal to the vkey key name). Key type 0x01, Ed25519. Checkpoint: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/checkpoint https://histor.modelmarket.dev/checkpoint Add-checkpoint rate: one request per witness every 10 minutes (144 per day), from a timer on the log's host. The log only grows; consistency proofs are served at https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/proof/consistency?first=&second= https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/log/proof/consistency?first=&second= and the witness client handles 409 by resubmitting from the witness's size. What it is: HISTOR is a public, append-only log of the tool definitions that MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers listed in the official MCP registry actually serve. Each observation becomes a signed statement, and the statements are leaves of an RFC 9162 Merkle tree (currently about 1.29 million leaves, growing by a few thousand a day). The format is specified at https://github.com/alexar76/histor/blob/main/docs/spec/transparency-log.md https://github.com/alexar76/histor/blob/main/docs/spec/transparency-log.md with test vectors; the checkpoint and note formats were checked byte for byte against golang.org/x/mod/sumdb/note and github.com/transparency-dev/formats/note. Intended users: MCP clients and security tooling that want to check whether the tool definitions they received are the ones the server serves to everyone (split-view detection for tool definitions), and researchers using the weekly open dataset (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512759). Witness cosignatures are what would turn the log's own signed heads into evidence that it shows everyone the same tree. Load versus utility: 144 add-checkpoint requests per witness per day, small checkpoints, no burst. Expected lifetime: long-lived; the log has run since September 2026 and the origin and key will not change. The same instance also implements c2sp.org/tlog-witness https://histor.modelmarket.dev/witness/v1, https://histor.modelmarket.dev/witness/v1, key histor.modelmarket.dev/witness+87df9f83+BHmgo3SRWlaehFMgWBDIOWFgs1V2YQS+ufBcg/ht6kw/). It is not offered as a network witness yet; if that would be useful once it has run for a while, I would be glad to discuss it separately. Thank you, Aleksandr Artamokhov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMCP servers · NicheDB (https://nichedb.dev/c/mcp?tag=tool-poisoning)\nciteturn22search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; HISTOR — public transparency log of MCP tool definitions: reads what remote MCP endpoints in the official registry advertise (never calls a tool), signs it as MTL/1 labels, appends them to an RFC 9162 Merkle log, and ans\n\nOct 21, 2026 10:20 AM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\nmcpgithubtopicmodel-context-protocolxiaoxusopjava★ 1\n\n  * Image\n\nchsxthwik/MCP-FSCK\n\nIntegrity check for MCP server configs audits Claude, Cursor, VS Code and Windsurf agent configs for secrets, injection-prone args, tool poisoning and dangerous capability combos.\n\nOct 13, 2026 8:34 PM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\ntopicchsxthwiktool-poisoningsecurity-auditmodel-context-protocolscanner★ 1\n\n  * Image\n\nalexar76/histor\n\nHISTOR — public transparency log of MCP tool definitions: reads what remote MCP endpoints in the official registry advertise (never calls a tool), signs it as MTL/1 labels, appends them to an RFC 9162 Merkle log, and ans\n\nNov 3, 2026 8:10 PM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\nmcpgithubtopicmodel-context-protocolalexar76python★ 1\n\n  * Image\n\ngostanos/smallprint-tools\n\nSmall Print's open tools: the disclosure lab, and soon the command, the MCP server and the plugin\n\nNov 6, 2026 5:51 AM · MCP servers tagged on GitHub · mcp-server · open ↗\n\nmcpgithubtopicmodel-context-protocolgostanospython★ 2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76 (Aleksandr Artamokhov) · GitHub (https://github.com/alexar76/)\nciteturn22search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; HISTOR  | MCP clients · Hub  | signed MTL/1 labels · `/check` · `histor.check@v1`\n10  | Watch  | SKOPOS the fleet · LOGOS the federation · Alien Monitor all of it in live 3D\n\nBelow, every repo appears in exactly one group — the role it plays in that pipeline. If you know only a name, jump to the A–Z index.\n\n🗺️ Ecosystem map — the money & trust spine as edges (click to expand)\n\n> The overview diagram is at the top of this README. GitHub Mermaid truncates node labels, so the map stays SVG. Zoomable 3D: Alien Monitor.\n\nFrom  | To  | Flow\n--- | --- | ---\nFactory / aicom  | HESTIA  | scaffold · signed deploy onto the hosted runtime\nHESTIA  | Hub  | explicit announce (hosting ≠ listing)\nTHEMIS  | Hub  | publish admission · approve / review / reject\nBASANOS  | ACEX / contracts  | Solidity scan · signed assurance pack\nDOLOS  | UNI Anvil (fork)  | dynamic EVM red team · Ed25519 findings\nOracles ×17  | Hub  | signed verifiable math\nGAIA  | Hub  | attested physical-world readings\nATLAS  | GAIA  | planetary sensor map over those readings\nCite desks  | ATLAS / Hub  | focused B2B evidence products that buy `atlas.*` / `gaia.*`\nMETIS  | Hub  | verify cognition\nHub ↔ ARGUS  | —  | invoke / settle · discover / pay / consume\nHub  | ACEX  | CapShares\nSKOPOS  | Factory / Hub  | fleet watch\nMOMUS  | Factory / Hub  | find · sign\nHISTOR  | MCP clients · Hub  | signed MTL/1 labels · `/check` · `histor.check@v1`\nTreasury  | Hub / chain  | pay on verify\nHub / MOMUS / SKOPOS / Treasury  | LOGOS  | read-only snapshots · anomalies · insights\nAlien Monitor  | Hub  | live 3D graph\n\nNew here? Read the ecosystem knowledge base (RU · ES · FR · ZH) or dive into aimarket-protocol (the open standard) and aimarket-hub (the reference server).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParaphern/rugsnare | Skillget (https://skillget.dev/listings/paraphern-rugsnare?lang=en)\nciteturn22search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * HISTOR (alexar76) — Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23512760, CC BY 4.0, monthly snapshots (also `alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions` on Hugging Face). ... Its README links our drift-watch corpus as a related dataset.\n## Independent measurements\n\nOur audit is not a lone number. Two third-party datasets — published with method and caveats, neither affiliated with us — land on the same shape from different angles:\n\n  * HISTOR (alexar76) — Zenodo DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23512760, CC BY 4.0, monthly snapshots (also `alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions` on Hugging Face). Every new npm/PyPI MCP-server release sandboxed and every registry remote read daily: 1,419 of 21,738 packages (6.5%) changed their `tools/list` in four weeks (24 Sep – 20 Oct 2026), and 4,926 of 10,929 remote servers (45%) served a different tool list at least once. It also documents 2,719 digit-only description changes — the churn that argues for a readable field-level diff over a single yes/no hash (discussion: modelcontextprotocol#3431). 26 of our 28 watch-corpus packages appear in the log; 8 changed in that window — consistent with our per-server pages.\n  * agent-skills-state-2026 (basitalisandhu) — repo. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo MCP logs matched 23 tools. Five days later, every name had changed. | AIWithGhost (https://aiwithghost.com/news/news-two-mcp-logs-matched-23-tools-five-days-later-every-name-had-changed/)\nciteturn22search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; HISTOR server history · LEGION server record ... Verify the STH and the three signed MTL/1 labels against HISTOR’s pinned `did:key`.\n\nDev.to Security 🔐 Cybersecurity Wed, 04 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0000 👁 0 📖 3 min read\n\n# Two MCP logs matched 23 tools. Five days later, every name had changed.\n\nCompare the latest saved entries for one MCP server and you get zero tool names in common. Compare the observations from the same evening and all 23 tool fingerprints match. We maintain HISTOR, a transparency log for th\n\nImage: Two MCP logs matched 23 tools. Five days later, every name had changed.\n\nCompare the latest saved entries for one MCP server and you get zero tool names in common. Compare the observations from the same evening and all 23 tool fingerprints match.\n\nWe maintain HISTOR, a transparency log for the tool definitions MCP servers advertise. We wanted to see whether an independent observer could corroborate one of our observations. We picked LEGION-tlog because it publishes a timestamp and a SHA-256 fingerprint for each tool. Those fingerprints let us compare individual definitions even when the logs use different formats for their records. LEGION developer documentation\n\n## What the two logs saw\n\nThe server was `com.aiapplyd/aiapplyd`. We compared the tool name, description and input schema using LEGION’s canonicalization rule for per-tool fingerprints. All times below are UTC in 2026. HISTOR server history · LEGION server record\n\nHISTOR observation  | What the comparison showed\n--- | ---\n19 October, 21:37:07  | All 23 fingerprints matched the LEGION observation from 22:01:05, about 24 minutes later.\n24 October, 18:19:21  | All 23 names switched from `aiapplyd_` to `getmattered_`. The suffixes matched the earlier names. The latest entry in our archived LEGION response still showed the October 19 observation.\n31 October, 12:45:49  | HISTOR recorded a set of 40 tools. None of their names appeared in the archived LEGION record. This is the set in the November 2 HISTOR snapshot.\n\nThe October 24 observation is easy to misread if you only look at counts. Both sets contain 23 tools, but a client using the earlier names would have to account for the name changes. The matching suffixes give you a way to line up the names. Whether the tools behave the same would take a separate test.\n\nThe evidence tells us when HISTOR first recorded the new set and which earlier version LEGION corroborated. It leaves open why the archived LEGION response had no later entry. In particular, the time of an observation and the time a server actually changed can differ.\n\n## Replay the check offline\n\nDownload the offline replay bundle.\n\nWith the case bundle unpacked, run this from its directory using Node.js 22.\n    \n    `node replay.mjs > replay-output.json\n    diff -u result.json replay-output.json\n    `\n\nAn empty diff means the replay reproduced the saved result. The script uses Node’s standard library and the bundled records, keys, signed tree heads (STHs) and inclusion proofs. It performs three checks.\n\n  1. LEGION-tlog. Verify the signing key against the pinned root key, verify the Ed25519 signatures on leaf 29030 and its STH, and recompute the path to the signed root of the 50,578-leaf Merkle tree.\n  2. HISTOR. Verify the STH and the three signed MTL/1 labels against HISTOR’s pinned `did:key`. Verify their inclusion in the 1,564,894-leaf Merkle tree and recompute the tool set digest bound to each label.\n  3. Comparison. Recompute the per-tool fingerprints from the authenticated HISTOR tool sets and compare them with the fingerprints in the LEGION record. The output lists names unique to either side and any shared names whose definitions differ.\n\nThe signed HISTOR label for the 40-tool set dates to October 31. The November 2 read time comes from snapshot metadata. The replay verifies inclusion under the bundled tree heads. Checking consistency across other tree heads or the behavior of the server would require separate evidence.\n\nFor a tool-pinning workflow, the case suggests a concrete diagnostic. When two logs disagree, put the observation timestamps next to the fingerprints and look for an earlier matching tool set. That can turn a generic mismatch into a dated, inspectable change.\n\nWe maintain HISTOR. AI assistance was used for the analysis, the replay code, the illustrations and the writing.\n\n📰 Read the original article on Dev.to Security\n\nOriginally published by Dev.to Security. Aggregated on AIWithGhost for educational purposes — full credit and traffic to the original publisher.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDataSet alternatives & news | Sanka Signal (https://sanka.com/signal/apps/dataset/)\nciteturn22search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Limitless enterprise infrastructure for live da